wn tiles (3 left). So it's possible. \n\nSo maybe discarding B2 is a good idea, as it's part of a potential chow. But if I discard it, someone might take it, which would help them but not me. However, if I keep it, I can form the chow later. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard B8, which is part of B7-B8-B9. If I discard B8, someone might take it. But again, depends on others' hands. \n\nAnother angle: looking at the game history, other players have played various tiles. For example, the next player has played F4, J2, W9, etc. The player across has played B3, B4, B5. So they have a chow in B3-B4-B5. The previous player has played T3, T9, etc. \n\nLooking at my own game history, I played W2, J2, T2, T5, AnGang W1, F2, B9, B5, Chi W6, etc. So I have some melds already. \n\nNow, considering the tiles I have left: W7, W8, T7, B1 (2), B2, B7, B8. \n\nIf I want to maximize my chances of forming a high-scoring hand, maybe I should focus on the W suit. Since I already have a kong and a chow in W, maybe trying to form another meld in W. \n\nIf I can get W7-W8-W9, but no. Or a pung of W7. Since I have one W7, and three left in the unshown, maybe it's worth keeping. So I shouldn't discard W7. \n\nSimilarly, W8 is part of a potential pung if I can get two more. But there are three W8 left. \n\nSo maybe I should discard a tile that's not part of the W suit. The options are T7, B1, B2, B7, B8. \n\nBetween these, which is the least useful? T7 is a single tile in another suit. If I discard T7, that's one less tile to worry about. Then I can focus on B1, B2, B7, B8. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard B2, which is part of a potential chow in B1-B2-B3. But if I discard B2, I still have two B1s. Maybe that's okay. \n\nBut if I discard T7, I remove a tile from another suit, which helps in reducing the number of suits. \n\nSo maybe the best move is to discard T7. That way, I eliminate a tile from the Bamboo suit, which is a separate suit from my W and Dots. This would help in aiming for Full Flu